## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis for dynamic frequency tracking, demonstrates how to monitor hot keys efficiently, and contrasts incremental updates with full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized analysis for batched updates
- Frequency buckets for O(1) max-frequency queries
- Hash maps for per-label counts
- Sparse vs. dense label domains
- Space-time tradeoff analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
class HotKeyTracker {
  constructor(initialCounts) {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.maxFreq = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(initialCounts)) {
      this.cnt.set(k, v);
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, v);
    }
  }

  update(label, delta) {
    const old = this.cnt.get(label) || 0;
    const freqCount = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
    if (freqCount === 1) this.freq.delete(old);
    else this.freq.set(old, freqCount - 1);

    const nw = old + delta;
    this.cnt.set(label, nw);
    this.freq.set(nw, (this.freq.get(nw) || 0) + 1);
    this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, nw);
  }

  hasHotKey(threshold) {
    return this.maxFreq >= threshold;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design incremental data structures that handle batch updates with strong worst-case guarantees, and to reason about amortized costs versus rebuilding from scratch.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with parity (union-find with bipartite tracking)
- Incremental graph property maintenance
- Amortized analysis (inverse Ackermann function)
- Batch processing optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
class ParityDSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.parity = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.isBipartite = true;
  }

  find(x) {
    if (this.parent[x] !== x) {
      const root = this.find(this.parent[x]);
      this.parity[x] ^= this.parity[this.parent[x]];
      this.parent[x] = root;
    }
    return this.parent[x];
  }

  union(x, y) {
    const rootX = this.find(x);
    const rootY = this.find(y);
    if (rootX === rootY) {
      if (this.parity[x] === this.parity[y]) {
        this.isBipartite = false;
      }
      return;
    }
    if (this.rank[rootX] < this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootX] = rootY;
      this.parity[rootX] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else if (this.rank[rootX] > this.rank[rootY]) {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    } else {
      this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
      this.rank[rootX]++;
      this.parity[rootY] = this.parity[x] ^ this.parity[y] ^ 1;
    }
  }

  processBatch(edges) {
    for (const [u, v] of edges) {
      this.union(u, v);
    }
    return this.isBipartite;
  }
}
```

## Comparison to Recomputing from Scratch
- **DSU with parity**: O(B α(n)) per batch, O(1) query
- **BFS/DFS recomputation**: O(n + m) per batch

The DSU approach provides a significant speedup when B << m, as it reuses information from previous batches rather than recomputing the entire graph structure.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of batch processing with dynamic statistics and tight performance guarantees, blending order-statistics trees with buffering for amortization analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics trees or multisets with subtree sizes and prefix sums
- Bulk-merge and batch amortization techniques
- Maintaining running sums to compute L1 deviations efficiently

## Code Example
```javascript
class TwoTierMedian {
  constructor() {
    this.committed = new OrderStatisticsTree();
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  insertBatch(batch) {
    this.buffer.push(...batch);
    this.buffer.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    this.committed.bulkMerge(this.buffer);
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  getMedian() {
    return this.committed.findByRank(Math.floor(this.committed.size / 2));
  }

  getSumAbsoluteDeviations() {
    const median = this.getMedian();
    return this.committed.sumAbsoluteDeviations(median);
  }
}
```

## Bulk-Merge and Rebalancing Strategy
Sort the buffer, then merge two sorted sequences into the OST using an in-order traversal. After merging, rebalance the tree (e.g., via treap randomization or AVL-style rotations) to maintain O(log n) operations.

## Amortized Per-Element Cost
Let B be the batch size. Sorting takes O(B log B), bulk-merge takes O(B), and rebalancing adds O(B). Amortized per element: O(log B) per batch element, which is optimal compared to O(log n) per individual insert.

## Memory Bounds
- OST stores all committed elements: O(n)
- Buffer stores one batch: O(B)
- Prefix sums at each node: O(n) additional memory

## Two Optimizations
1. **Lazy Buffering**: Accumulate multiple batches before merging to reduce merge frequency
2. **Hybrid Selection**: Use quickselect on the buffer first to find median bounds before querying the OST

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming frequency sketches are common in prod telemetry. This question probes knowledge of CMS, accuracy guarantees, batch processing, and amortized space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Count-Min Sketch (CMS)
- ε, δ error bounds
- Update/query complexity for batches
- Space complexity O(wd)
- Trade-offs vs exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
// CMS skeleton
const w = Math.ceil(Math.E / eps);
const d = Math.ceil(Math.log(1/delta));
const table = Array.from({length: d}, () => Array(w).fill(0));

function update(x) { for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]++; }

function query(x) { let m = Infinity; for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) m = Math.min(m, table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]); return m; }
```

